memory is the cheekiest most tenacious  little boy that I think I've ever met  when Lauren's son Henry was born doctors  were unable to diagnose just what was  wrong it was 20 months before a  groundbreaking DNA test found he had  Costello syndrome a rare disease that  makes him susceptible to childhood  cancers it was like a massive weight off  our shoulders as in we finally had an  idea of what Henry's life might look  like and why he behaved the way he did  the unknown is so much worse than the  known so by having that allowed us to  have a better understanding and  acceptance really of what life might be  like for Henry the new fast-track DNA  test which diagnosed Henry was developed  by a team of scientists in Exeter a  single test which looks for genetic  mutations in a child's DNA as part of  this new test DNA samples are taken to  this machine it helps to diagnose from  around 5,000 different rare conditions a  process which used to take 10 days is  now done in 27 hours it's now being  rolled out across the NHS since being  introduced in October 2000 and 1980  babies and children have received the  new tests of those almost half were  given a diagnosis for their rare disease  for the first time up to 700 babies and  children are expected to benefit each  year from the tests which can cut  diagnosis times from weeks to days it's  a huge step because when genetic tests  had started in the NHS about 25 years  ago looking for rare diseases such as  cystic fibrosis we had to set up each  test for each disease so in fact we only  were able to test for the most common of  the rare diseases and today that doesn't  matter you know turn the page good boy a  single quicker test means children  suffering from some of the rarest  diseases could now get a quicker  diagnosis and in turn quicker treatment  Dan Whitehead Sky News in Exeter
 

